Research Question
How have the import and export quantities and values of various food and agricultural items in United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland evolved from 1961 to 2022, and what significant trends can be observed in their trade patterns?
Data Acquisition: 
The Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations (FAO) compiled the dataset utilized in this analysis. The data is available for download from the FAOSTAT database, specifically at the following link: https://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#data/TCL
Compilation, processing, and dissemination by the Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO).
Detailed information regarding the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland of agricultural prod-ucts is included in the Food and Agricultural Trade Dataset of the Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO). It encompasses annual trade data from 1961 to 2022, which pertains to approximately 600 distinct food and agricultural commodities. The dataset offers valuable insights into global trade patterns and is comprehen-sive, comparable, and current.

Numerous agricultural product categories that were irrelevant to my research were encountered during the selection of this dataset from the FAO website. I eliminated these useless items and selected data that was specifically relevant to the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland in order simplify the dataset and concentrate on the most important information.
• Export Quantity: The physical quantity of domestically produced or manufactured goods that are export-ed from the country. Live animals are expressed in units, while many commodities are expressed in tons.
• Export Value: Expressed in thousand US dollars, reported as FOB (free on board) which includes the value of the goods plus the services performed to deliver the goods to the border of the exporting country.
• Import Quantity: Physical quantity of products imported for domestic consumption or processing, includ-ing re-imports. Expressed in tonnes for most commodities and in units for live animals.
• Import Value: Expressed in thousand US dollars, reported as CIF (Cost, Insurance, and Freight), includ-ing the value of the goods, the services to deliver goods to the border of the exporting country, and the ser-vices to deliver the goods to the importing country’s border.

Preparation: 

The columns which are deleted
To simplify the dataset and concentrate on the research question, I eliminated numerous columns that were irrelevant and introduced unnecessary complexity. The following columns have been removed: Domain Code, Domain, Area Code (M49), Area, Element Code, Item Code (CPC), Year Code, Flag, Flag Description, and Note. The Domain column was eliminated since I had specifically chosen data that was associated with agricultural products, rendering it unnecessary to determine whether it was agricultural or not. The Area and Area Code columns were eliminated because all data is relevant to the region of Great Britain and Northern Ireland, and therefore, there is no requirement to specify the location. The Flag and Note columns were eliminated because they contain descriptions of the data collection process that are not pertinent to the research question. Furthermore, the dataset already includes other columns that contain the requisite information, so all code-related columns were eliminated.
The columns which are retained:
The dataset retains the following columns: Element, Item, Year, Unit, and Value. Due to its listing of agricultural and food imports and exports, the Element column is maintained. The Item column is retained because it contains a comprehensive list of all agricultural and food products. The Year column is essential because it encompasses data from 1961 to 2022. The Unit column is crucial because it specifies the units in which items are exported and imported, specifically in tons and (1000) USD. Lastly, the Value column is maintained due to its comprehensive listing of the values of all agricultural and food products that are imported and exported.
	
