The proportion of sum of Export Quantity and Value of items throughout the whole tenure.
   

From 1961 to 2022, wheat accounted for 59.11% (103.79 million tons) of the total exports from the UK, making it the most frequently exported agricultural item. This is illustrated in the first pie chart. In contrast, the second pie chart indicates that cigarettes are the most exported product, contributing 20.77% ($31 million) of the total export value, despite not being the most exported by volume. Other high-value items include wine, meat of cattle, and chocolate products. Wheat, despite its substantial export volume, only accounts for 12.4% of the total export value. This discrepancy suggests that, even though wheat is essential to the UK's agricultural export sector due to its favourable production conditions, high-revenue exports are derived from value-added or high-priced items, such as cigarettes, which have higher unit values.
The proportion of sum of Import Quantity and Value of items throughout the whole tenure.
 
The initial pie chart illustrates that wheat was the most frequently imported agricultural item in the United Kingdom from 1961 to 2022, comprising 26.88% (134.8 million tons) of the total imports. Wine (8.97%), bananas (7.56%), and maize (corn) (7.32%) are additional substantial imports in terms of quantity. Conversely, the second pie chart indicates that wine is the most valuable import, accounting for 34.83% ($130 million) of the total import value. Chocolate products (11.91%) and chicken meat (9.98%) follow in that order. Despite wheat's dominance in import quantity, it only accounts for 6.16% of the total import value, underscoring that high-volume imports such as wheat are not the most revenue-generating materials. This discrepancy suggests that the import value is significantly influenced by factors such as market demand and unit price, with high-value items such as wine and chocolate products contributing significantly to import expenses. 

