The charts he provided illustrate the import quantities and values of the top five agricultural items in the UK from 1961 to 2022, indicating substantial trends. Since the late 1970s, wheat has experienced a substantial decline in quantity, stabilizing at lower levels, while its value remains less prominent. Initially, wheat was a dominant import. Contrary to this, wine has experienced a substantial increase in both import quantity and value, particularly since the 1990s, which is indicative of its high market value and high demand. Even though import quantities have remained consistent, chocolate products have experienced a substantial in-crease in import value since 2000, underscoring their high unit value. Maize and bananas exhibit volatility in their import quantities, with substantial peaks, and their values also increase, suggesting that demand and supply conditions are subject to fluctuations. The demand for meat products, particularly those derived from cattle and chickens, has been consistently increasing, as evidenced by the consistent increase in their im-port values. Overall, the charts suggest that the UK's agricultural imports are shifting toward high-value items such as wine, chocolate, and meat, which is indicative of evolving consumer preferences and market dynamics. Conversely, the volume of imported wheat has decreased.
